[Music]  good morning ladies and gentlemen  i'm malcolm rivkind i have been invited  to take  the session that we're about to begin uh  perhaps i can just begin if i may  just by explaining as far as i have a  competence  to speak on the subject before us what  that competence is  i served for a total of six years in the  british foreign office as minister of  state and then as foreign secretary  and was also defense secretary this was  during the governments of  margaret thatcher and john major and in  the early session as a junior minister  as minister of state  i was responsible under the prime  minister and the foreign secretary  for our relations with the soviet union  and was involved in  uh margaret thatcher's meeting with mr  gorbachev the famous meeting  when she said he is a man with whom we  can do  business uh so i was able to see it  firsthand  uh the chemistry of that meeting and  what it then led to  and the subject is the uh is the  end of history what happened in 1989  and in the immediate period there after  and that phrase the end of history  it wasn't just invented it was a an  american academic  professor fukuyama who suggested that so  crucial  were the events of those years that it  really was the end of history because it  was a triumph  of the west the triumph of democracy and  of the values that we have  and the issue that we are discussing  this morning and which others have  discussed  is whether that was a wise thing for him  to say  or whether the events that we have seen  in the last 20 to 30 years uh suggests  not that it wasn't important  um but that uh in some respects we're  going back or have gone back  to the bad old days so i begin by  just emphasizing the significance of  what happened in 1989  and his extraordinary coincidence that  that of course was a hundred years  after the fall of the bastille which  began the french revolution  so in 1989 was not one event it was  actually  three historic events all connected with  each other  but all nevertheless distinct in a way  that i will explain very briefly  first of all it was the end of the cold  war and the cold war was that  geopolitical struggle between the soviet  union  and the west particularly the united  states and it could at any stage have  led from a cold war to a hot war  a third world war and if it had done of  course it would have had the added  horror  of nuclear weapons and it would have  been the possibility of a nuclear  exchange which could have  literally destroyed all human life and  the planet which we are part  so the cold war came to an end  and whatever problems we have with mr  putin today and we have serious problems  they are not of the same order because  uh  in the case of the soviet union it was  at one period the military equal  of the united states which russia  certainly is not it was economically  strong at one stage which russia is  not and of course it had global  aspirations whereas mr putin  is more of an opportunist uh rather than  someone seeking to dominate  the world so the end of the cold war  was the first dramatic change and of  course the cold war immediately followed  the second world war  and therefore europe was beginning to  get back to some degree of normality  so i said there were three things that  happened that was the first  the second simultaneously but in a  different way in terms of the detail  was it was the great end of the  ideological struggle  between communism and capitalism between  communism and market economics  because there is no communist society  today with a mild exception of north  korea but even that  is basically a totalitarian despotism  china is still run by a communist party  as  is vietnam and cuba but their societies  are not communist  in any marxist leninist sense in the  case of china you essentially  have state capitalism and why did  communism collapse why did it fail  essentially because it couldn't deliver  economically  what mr gorbachev when he came to  britain  most noted was how when he was shown a  supermarket  it wasn't a supermarket for the  privileged for the members of the  political elite  it had a marvelous extraordinary display  of choice and this was  for ordinary members of the public  everyone was able to do their shopping  in that way and that was just one  example  of the failure of communism somebody had  remarked  that communism only worked either in  heaven  where they did not need it or in hell  where they had it  already that might have been a bit  unkind but it summed up  uh what could be described so communism  failed as a system and of course what  you saw after the  1989 was not just introduction of  capitalism and market economics  throughout eastern europe and poland and  hungary  czech republic the baltic states but  even russia itself  uh through the oligarchs it's not the  kind of capitalism  that we have in britain but it  nevertheless is closer to capitalism  than it ever was to socialism or  communism and that is something  irreversible  so that's the second big change that  happened at that time  first the end of the cold war the second  uh the  collapse of communism as an alternative  global ideology and it was global  because it had many adherents in other  parts of the world and  for many years during the cold war the  french and italian communist parties  were the largest political parties in  france in  italy although they never got far now  i come to the third and it's the third  big change  that is explains a lot of the problems  we are living with  at the moment because the third dramatic  change  the one that was least predicted even in  the timescale we're talking about was  the collapse of  europe's last empire the russian  empire the british empire had gone  gradually over the years has had the  french  the spanish the portuguese the belgian  the dutch and so forth all had  disappeared  the russian empire was the last to go  and i'm not just talking about the end  of the warsaw pact and  countries like poland and hungary and so  forth are becoming free again and  becoming  essentially central europe i'm actually  talking about the implosion  of the soviet union itself the state  called the soviet union previously  russia  disintegrated into 15 or 16 separate  independent countries russia was the  largest  ukraine was the second largest and then  you have the baltic states  states of the caucasus georgia armenia  azerbaijan  the central asian states kazakhstan  uzbekistan several others  and so forth so this was something  totally  uh unexpected even to the russians  themselves  but it happened and of course the  consequence of that  is that if for example physical war were  ever to begin  between russia and the west it would not  begin  in the center of germany where it would  have been up till 1989 with  half of berlin controlled ultimately by  the soviet union  russia's borders are now very very far  to the east  they would have to go through belarus  they'd have to go through the baltic  states  or ukraine before they even got to  poland and still  some way so from a conventional point of  view  russia no longer can be seen as a threat  in the way that the soviet union was  now mr putin's statement the most  important statement i think he's made  was before he even became president  of russia when he said that the greatest  geopolitical disaster of his lifetime  was the collapse  of the soviet uh state because he didn't  mean communism he meant the russian  empire  and because right from today's borders  of  russia you have to go back to peter the  great in the 17th century  um towards the end of the 17th century  when our borders  were as far from the rest of europe as  they are  today and that's why he is determined  to uh limit the independence of ukraine  if possible to reabsorb them into the  russian embrace  while he tries to destabilize the baltic  states and so forth  so you had these three massive changes  and there's no doubt  that the immediate effect of that in the  early 1990s  was that communism had disappeared  the soviet union was no longer a threat  and needed its leaders gorbachev and  then yeltsin  wanted to become closer to the west and  looked at one stage as if that could  lead  to russia as well as eastern europe  adopting a parliamentary system the rule  of law  becoming more integrated into the  european family  of nations so that you know these were  pretty impressive things  but what we have saw thereafter uh and  it didn't take long for it to begin to  happen you started off with the united  states  becoming the sole superpower indeed so  powerful was america that it wasn't as a  superpower it was a super duper bar  the only one we actually had far more  powerful than  russia now was china was no longer it  was not yet  uh as powerful as it is today and so  that looked as if the united states  really was a pax americana that would  determine the fortunes of the world  but that only lasted a few years i began  by quoting professor fukuyama who said  it was the end  of history that was rather optimistic  i believe in an alternative phrase as  one door closes  another slams in your face because it  didn't take long  for things to begin happening in europe  that were very very unpleasant  very very disagreeable which we thought  might have gone forever  we got the return of xenophobic  nationalism  particularly for example in the balkans  where yugoslavia  has collapsed disintegrated into  half a dozen separate states and the  most serious consequence was the bosnian  effectively civil war when bosnian serbs  bosnian kurtz bosnian muslims  all fought each other we had ethnic  cleansing for several years  and that was something that europe  thought would never occur ending with  the  ghastliness of the srebrenica massacre  but it wasn't just in yugoslavia  because also within russia itself to  continue watching this video  click the link in the top left or in the